The implementation of the `IOUtils.skip()` method in Apache Commons Compress library is as follows:

```java
public static long skip(InputStream input, long numToSkip) throws IOException {
    long available = numToSkip;
    while (numToSkip > 0) {
        long skipped = input.skip(numToSkip);
        if (skipped == 0) {
            break;
        }
        numToSkip -= skipped;
    }

    return available - numToSkip;
}
```

Based on the code snippet, it is evident that the implementation of `IOUtils.skip()` iterates over the input stream and attempts to skip the specified number of bytes. It breaks out of the loop if the `skip` operation returns 0 before the requested number of bytes have been skipped.

The issue reported indicates that this behavior is not in line with the contract specified in the Javadoc, which states that the method should only skip less than the requested number of bytes if the end of the input stream has been reached. However, the current implementation of `IOUtils.skip()` does not strictly adhere to this contract.

Therefore, the likely root cause of the problem reported is the premature break in the loop when `skip` returns 0, instead of continuing until the end of the stream is reached.

To address this issue, the `IOUtils.skip()` method needs to be updated to skip fewer bytes only when the end of the input stream has been reached, as indicated in the Javadoc contract.

Here are the methods that need to be fixed:
```
org.apache.commons.compress.utils.IOUtils.skip(java.io.InputStream, long)
```

